meta {
  name: Sign In With Apple
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/apple
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "identityToken": "paste-a-real-identity-token-here",
    "nonce": "paste-the-raw-nonce-here",
    "fullName": {
      "givenName": "Bruno",
      "familyName": "Tester"
    },
    "email": "bruno@privaterelay.appleid.com"
  }
}

vars:post-response {
  accessToken: res.body.accessToken
  refreshToken: res.body.refreshToken
  userId: res.body.user.id
}

docs {
  Needs a real Apple identity token, which only a device can produce. Print
  both values from the onCompletion handler in WelcomeView and paste them
  here. The token is rejected after 10 minutes (maxTokenAge), so work quickly.

  Send the RAW nonce, not the hash. iOS hashes it before handing it to Apple;
  the server hashes what you send here and compares against the hash Apple
  echoed into the token. That binding is what stops a token from one sign-in
  being replayed into another.

  Expect 200 for a returning user, or 201 for a new account.

  `fullName` and `email` in this body are untrusted hints, used only to name a
  brand-new account. Identity always comes from the verified token's `sub`.
  Editing them cannot make you sign in as anyone else — try it.

  Apple returns fullName and email only on the FIRST authorization. To get
  them again, revoke the app under Settings > Apple ID > Sign in with Apple.

  If the response carries "emailConflict": true, the token's email already
  belonged to a password account, so a SEPARATE account was created rather
  than linking the two. See the comment in routes/auth.ts for why neither
  linking nor a 409 is safe there.
}
